fix(pip): Expose the lens like the main view, and build the lens camera at the authored FOV

Four things, all of them from reading the pack and the engine rather than guessing. The two the
player can see are the dark lens and the magnification.

Exposure (the dark lens). The image dumps settle what this was: the lens set renders the right
scene, its accumulator is bright (115/18/109) and its combined image averages 8/255 against
40/255 in the main view of the same scene. The exposure is a 1x1 value produced by phase_luminance
through the post process chain, and phase_combine returns for the lens before that chain - by
design, the display sized stages and the upscaler are main pass work - so the lens set's pool stays
at the zero it was created with and the combine multiplied a correct image by it. The lens now
takes the main set's current pool. Which binding carries it is the part that is easy to get wrong,
and the review caught it: r2_types.h names $user$tonemap_src and $user$tonemap, blender_luminance
reads _src, and blender_combine binds the combine's s_tonemap to r2_RT_luminance_cur, i.e.
t_LUM_DEST and rt_LUM_pool[1]. The first version of this fix bound t_LUM_src and pool[0], which
would not have changed anything visible.

Magnification. The camera used to be the main projection scaled by (g_fov / lensFov); the packs
state the sight's own lens FOV in degrees and the power as g_fov / scope_lens_fov, and
build_projection() is built from tan(fov/2), so a 21.667 degree sight was rendered at 24.0 degrees,
about 10 percent less magnification than the pack authors, and the ratio also inherited whatever
FOV the main view's ADS animation happened to be at. The lens projection is now rebuilt from the
authored angle, mirroring build_projection (xray's fFOV is the vertical one, _22 = 1/tan(fov/2) and
_11 = aspect * that, with the aspect taken from the main projection itself). Camera, the pack's
formula and this engine's own scope_lense_main_view binder, which computes fFOV / lensFov, now
agree exactly. r__svp_lens_power keeps its meaning: 200 percent is still twice the power.

Sampling mapping: identity, with the exact derivation now that the camera is exact. Looking through
an aperture of power mag, the eye at apparent angle t sees the world at t / mag, so the aperture
pixel at screen NDC n shows the direction n * (fFOV / 2) / mag; the lens camera, whose FOV is
lensFov = fFOV / mag, puts that direction at NDC n again, so the sample is the pixel's own uv. The
full derived matrix attempts measured degenerate (sampled spans of 1.5, 0.62 and then 0, the last
one showing a single texel over the whole aperture). The reference only needs a real matrix because
it captures a cropped sub rect of a display sized texture, which this engine does not do. The
reproject probe now checks the identity directly.

Heat vision: two corrections. CBlender_heatvision has TWO elements (0 copy_nomsaa, 1 heatvision),
so ps_r2_heatvision was the right index all along and the earlier E[0] change was a misdiagnosis
that would have turned a device's heat vision into a copy. It is reverted. And the lens pass must
not run that pass for a thermal optic at all: the shader is a device effect whose
compute_lens_mask() accepts only 1/2/4 tube layouts and returns 0 everywhere otherwise, so with no
worn device the whole frame comes out black - which is what a thermal sight was showing, and also
what the earlier forced r__heatvision was showing. A thermal OPTIC needs the full frame GBuffer
thermal image the pack's own shader builds for its 3DSS branch (infrared() over
gbuffer_load_data); the engine has no shader for it yet, so until that is written a thermal optic
shows the ordinary, correctly exposed lens image instead of a black one, and the log says so.

Diagnostics, so the next report can be measured instead of argued with: the per optic line now
prints the power the engine picked (mag, from the value the shaders see as current_zoom), the
pack's declared ends (fovMax / fovBase), the step count, the engine's own ADS FOV it fed the
dynamic branch, and which end the clamp chose or had to rescue.